WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.734 --> 00:00:01.667
-Hey, guys!

00:00:01.667 --> 00:00:04.801
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:04.801 --> 00:00:06.567
-I know.
-Wow.

00:00:06.567 --> 00:00:09.100
-We are so --
It's so great to see you.

00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:10.934
You look fantastic.
Thanks for coming back.

00:00:10.934 --> 00:00:12.100
-Thank you so much.

00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:13.901
That was really nice, guys,
by the way.

00:00:13.901 --> 00:00:17.801
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:17.801 --> 00:00:20.100
-There's such a buzz
around the whole block

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:21.834
in New York City,
just the fact that you're here.

00:00:21.834 --> 00:00:23.133
We're so excited.
-Really? Aww.

00:00:23.133 --> 00:00:24.734
-Well, yeah. We've got to talk
about "Midnights."

00:00:24.734 --> 00:00:26.067
Congrats on this!
-Thanks.

00:00:26.067 --> 00:00:32.734
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:32.734 --> 00:00:35.601
-I just want to break down
a couple of things.

00:00:35.601 --> 00:00:36.934
In just one day of sales,

00:00:36.934 --> 00:00:40.300
it became the top-selling album
of the year already.

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:42.300
Yeah, in just one day of sales.

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:43.434
Of the whole year.

00:00:43.434 --> 00:00:44.534
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:44.534 --> 00:00:46.601
"Midnights"
broke Spotify's record

00:00:46.601 --> 00:00:48.901
for most streams in a day.

00:00:48.901 --> 00:00:51.634
Today the top 10 songs
on Apple Music

00:00:51.634 --> 00:00:54.868
and Spotify
are from "Midnights."

00:00:54.868 --> 00:00:56.868
You can plug your ears because
I'm going to just keep going.

00:00:56.868 --> 00:00:59.901
-No, I like it too much.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:59.901 --> 00:01:01.334
-"Rolling Stone"
called "Midnights"

00:01:01.334 --> 00:01:04.367
an instant classic
in their five-star review.

00:01:04.367 --> 00:01:05.434
"Rolling Stone"!

00:01:05.434 --> 00:01:09.801
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:09.801 --> 00:01:13.767
And right now, this album --
I know.

00:01:13.767 --> 00:01:16.367
This album is on track to sell
well over one million

00:01:16.367 --> 00:01:17.701
copies in its first week.

00:01:17.701 --> 00:01:18.634
That is history, buddy.

00:01:18.634 --> 00:01:19.901
Congratulations.

00:01:19.901 --> 00:01:20.868
Unbelievable.

00:01:20.868 --> 00:01:23.534
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:23.534 --> 00:01:25.467
-I -- Yeah.
-How are you feeling?

00:01:25.467 --> 00:01:26.934
Is it a lot?
Is it exciting?

00:01:26.934 --> 00:01:30.767
Are you freaking?
-I'm beside myself really.

00:01:30.767 --> 00:01:34.267
Yeah, it's actually --
It's a concept record,

00:01:34.267 --> 00:01:35.467
but it's my first

00:01:35.467 --> 00:01:37.634
directly autobiographical album
in a while.

00:01:37.634 --> 00:01:39.667
Because the last album
that I put out

00:01:39.667 --> 00:01:44.234
was a rerecord of my album
"Red," so that has some space.

00:01:44.234 --> 00:01:47.267
You know, I wrote that stuff
a decade ago.

00:01:47.267 --> 00:01:49.701
"Folklore" and "Evermore,"
I was -- It was like story time.

00:01:49.701 --> 00:01:50.801
It was like mythology.

00:01:50.801 --> 00:01:52.367
Like, I'm creating a character.

00:01:52.367 --> 00:01:54.634
They went and did this
and felt this way.

00:01:54.634 --> 00:01:57.834
So, I'm feeling like --
I'm feeling very overwhelmed

00:01:57.834 --> 00:02:01.834
by the fans' love
for the record.

00:02:01.834 --> 00:02:04.367
I'm also feeling, like,
very soft and fragile.

00:02:04.367 --> 00:02:05.934
[ Laughter ]
So it's --

00:02:05.934 --> 00:02:07.801
-It's everything.
-The two can exist at once.

00:02:07.801 --> 00:02:09.734
But the fact that the fans
have done this --

00:02:09.734 --> 00:02:12.434
like,
the breaking of the records

00:02:12.434 --> 00:02:15.367
and the going out to the stores
and getting it.

00:02:15.367 --> 00:02:17.200
It's like, you know, I'm 32.

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:20.067
So, we're considered
geriatric pop stars.

00:02:20.067 --> 00:02:21.467
[ Laughter ]
-No, I don't think so.

00:02:21.467 --> 00:02:24.367
-They start trying to put us
out to pasture at age 25.

00:02:24.367 --> 00:02:25.634
I'm just happy to be here.

00:02:25.634 --> 00:02:26.868
-No, no, no.

00:02:26.868 --> 00:02:30.534
[ Cheers and applause ]
That's so funny.

00:02:30.534 --> 00:02:32.400
Oh, you're just getting
starting, buddy.

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:34.033
You're just getting started.

00:02:34.033 --> 00:02:38.167
You said a lot of this album
is made out of 13 stories,

00:02:38.167 --> 00:02:40.267
13 sleepless nights
throughout your life.

00:02:40.267 --> 00:02:43.567
-Yeah.
-Is it tough --

00:02:43.567 --> 00:02:44.934
I mean, I don't know
how you do it.

00:02:44.934 --> 00:02:47.501
Do you just get up and go --
in the middle of your --

00:02:47.501 --> 00:02:48.934
[ Laughter ]
Or, you know, record something

00:02:48.934 --> 00:02:50.267
on your phone
or you jot something down.

00:02:50.267 --> 00:02:51.934
Or like, "this is something,
I don't even know

00:02:51.934 --> 00:02:53.400
if it's good --
I'm blurting it out."

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:55.667
Or you do go like,
"Oh, this has a good hook"?

00:02:55.667 --> 00:02:58.000
-I kind of do all of that.

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:04.000
I love writing songs, poems,
stories, scripts.

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:07.767
I love writing those things
because there's a part of it

00:03:07.767 --> 00:03:10.133
that I don't quite understand
how it comes to fruition.

00:03:10.133 --> 00:03:12.567
There's something so mysterious
about writing.

00:03:12.567 --> 00:03:14.534
But I found that the more --

00:03:14.534 --> 00:03:17.067
the more I write,
the more I keep writing.

00:03:17.067 --> 00:03:18.400
I don't know what's been
going on.

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:20.667
But in the last six
or seven years, I've just --

00:03:20.667 --> 00:03:23.234
I've just been constantly
making things.

00:03:23.234 --> 00:03:26.133
And the more things I make,
the happier I am.

00:03:26.133 --> 00:03:27.267
-Yeah.

00:03:27.267 --> 00:03:28.667
-So, I've just continued
to do it.

00:03:28.667 --> 00:03:30.801
I'm just happy you guys
like that.

00:03:30.801 --> 00:03:32.033
-No, we love it.

00:03:32.033 --> 00:03:35.234
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:35.234 --> 00:03:37.167
-So, yeah, some of them --

00:03:37.167 --> 00:03:39.734
sometimes it feels
completely involuntary.

00:03:39.734 --> 00:03:44.334
Sometimes, like, your day is
completely interrupted by a hook

00:03:44.334 --> 00:03:48.467
or a lyric or just a thing
on piano or you know.

00:03:48.467 --> 00:03:50.834
Sometimes you don't know what
the place for it is going to be.

00:03:50.834 --> 00:03:53.534
But I just am always reaching
for my phone

00:03:53.534 --> 00:03:54.734
and recording things.

00:03:54.734 --> 00:03:57.234
-And do you know, like,
do you know "Mastermind"

00:03:57.234 --> 00:03:58.968
was going to be the 13th track?

00:03:58.968 --> 00:04:00.734
-Yeah, yeah.
-Wow.

00:04:00.734 --> 00:04:03.968
That's meta.
That's meta right there.

00:04:03.968 --> 00:04:05.567
That is so "Mastermind."

00:04:05.567 --> 00:04:06.667
[ Laughter ]

00:04:06.667 --> 00:04:08.534
-I think over the years
my fans and I,

00:04:08.534 --> 00:04:11.834
we kind of have
a really funny sort of --

00:04:11.834 --> 00:04:13.100
like, they tease me a lot.

00:04:13.100 --> 00:04:16.701
And I enjoy it.
-You do, yeah.

00:04:16.701 --> 00:04:20.000
-And one of the things --
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:22.033
One of the things they like
to make fun of me for

00:04:22.033 --> 00:04:28.667
is the kind of cryptic needing
to plan things out.

00:04:28.667 --> 00:04:30.100
And, like, I think we've
just stopped

00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:31.901
pretending it's accidental
at this point.

00:04:31.901 --> 00:04:33.734
[ Laughter ]
-No, that was fun.

00:04:33.734 --> 00:04:35.234
Now it's part of --
-We've stopped.

00:04:35.234 --> 00:04:36.501
-It's almost like you should
just write like five albums

00:04:36.501 --> 00:04:37.901
just for one album.

00:04:37.901 --> 00:04:39.000
'Cause you're like,
"No, this has to mean this,

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:40.734
and this has to --"
The way you listed

00:04:40.734 --> 00:04:43.934
just the names
of the tracks was so exciting.

00:04:43.934 --> 00:04:45.067
We were freaking out.

00:04:45.067 --> 00:04:46.200
-Thank you for saying
that was exciting.

00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:48.734
I was hoping people were like --
-I loved it!

00:04:48.734 --> 00:04:52.000
[ Cheers and applause ]
It was so fun.

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:54.000
With the bingo balls
going around.

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:55.734
-Yeah, I posted videos
on TikTok of me

00:04:55.734 --> 00:04:59.300
with a vintage bingo cage,
like with the handle.

00:04:59.300 --> 00:05:04.234
And I just announced track --
you know, one at a time.

00:05:04.234 --> 00:05:06.200
-And people, including me,
I go,

00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:07.801
"Oh, I think that will be
my favorite song."

00:05:07.801 --> 00:05:09.701
[ Laughter ]
Without even hearing the song.

00:05:09.701 --> 00:05:13.267
-It's so cool to hear you say
that because that was just me,

00:05:13.267 --> 00:05:17.067
you know, I don't know,
with my phone and a bingo cage.

00:05:17.067 --> 00:05:19.901
-On this one, you collaborate
with some very fun people.

00:05:19.901 --> 00:05:23.234
I saw Zoe Kravitz.
-Yeah.

00:05:23.234 --> 00:05:24.901
-We love her.
-Yes.

00:05:24.901 --> 00:05:26.100
-Lana Del Rey.
-Yes.

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:28.334
-Amazing.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:28.334 --> 00:05:32.634
And then I saw on drums --
is it Dylan O'Brien?

00:05:32.634 --> 00:05:33.767
-Dylan O'Brien.

00:05:33.767 --> 00:05:36.234
[ Cheers and applause ]
-I'm sorry.

00:05:36.234 --> 00:05:38.601
So, how -- Is he a drummer even?

00:05:38.601 --> 00:05:39.834
-He is.
-Wow.

00:05:39.834 --> 00:05:41.667
-And, you know,
not a lot of people know that.

00:05:41.667 --> 00:05:44.100
-I did not know that.
-And now they do.

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:45.534
But I like -- I really --

00:05:45.534 --> 00:05:47.367
I love to make things
with my friends.

00:05:47.367 --> 00:05:48.868
I love to work with my friends.

00:05:48.868 --> 00:05:51.734
I think that the experience
of making something

00:05:51.734 --> 00:05:56.067
is just as important as how
proud you are of it in the end.

00:05:56.067 --> 00:05:58.868
And I think it really informs
how proud I am of something

00:05:58.868 --> 00:06:02.067
if I had a really joyful
experience making something.

00:06:02.067 --> 00:06:05.968
This is a pretty dark album,
but I'd say I had more fun

00:06:05.968 --> 00:06:08.300
making it than any album
I've ever made.

00:06:08.300 --> 00:06:09.701
-Isn't that interesting?
-It's --

00:06:09.701 --> 00:06:12.400
Because I don't think that art
and suffering have to

00:06:12.400 --> 00:06:15.801
be holding hands all the time.
I think you can --

00:06:15.801 --> 00:06:18.534
I think you can write songs
about pain or grief

00:06:18.534 --> 00:06:19.701
or suffering or loss

00:06:19.701 --> 00:06:21.901
or hard things
that you go through in life.

00:06:21.901 --> 00:06:24.133
Shame, you know -- love to write
about that one.

00:06:24.133 --> 00:06:26.033
[ Laughter ]
Self-loathing.

00:06:26.033 --> 00:06:27.267
I could go on.

00:06:27.267 --> 00:06:30.567
But I think,
you know, with time

00:06:30.567 --> 00:06:32.501
and with the more albums
I put out,

00:06:32.501 --> 00:06:35.834
making albums and making things
and writing things feels

00:06:35.834 --> 00:06:39.300
like a way to sort of suck
the poison out of a snakebite.

00:06:39.300 --> 00:06:40.467
-Yeah.
-You're just like --

00:06:40.467 --> 00:06:41.701
-Get it out.
-You're putting it

00:06:41.701 --> 00:06:43.267
in the music,
you're putting it in a song.

00:06:43.267 --> 00:06:46.968
And it could be the most
harrowing song

00:06:46.968 --> 00:06:49.567
you have ever heard,
but we had a blast making it.

00:06:49.567 --> 00:06:51.601
-Good.
-And with "Snow on the Beach,"

00:06:51.601 --> 00:06:54.968
which features
the genius Lana Del Rey --

00:06:54.968 --> 00:06:56.567
-Yes.
[ Audience cheers ]

00:06:56.567 --> 00:07:00.667
-Yeah, you should --
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:00.667 --> 00:07:03.334
So, very lucky to have
collaborated with her on that.

00:07:03.334 --> 00:07:06.601
And Dylan was actually
in the studio with me and Jack

00:07:06.601 --> 00:07:08.000
because we were --

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:11.000
A lot of the time
we record at his place.

00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:13.367
And Dylan was just hanging out,
like, drinking wine with us

00:07:13.367 --> 00:07:14.634
and listening to stuff.

00:07:14.634 --> 00:07:16.968
And he was just trying out
the drum kit there.

00:07:16.968 --> 00:07:20.200
He wasn't -- He wasn't serious.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:20.200 --> 00:07:22.501
But we -- But we were --
-That's how it happens.

00:07:22.501 --> 00:07:24.000
-As I said,
we were drinking wine.

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:26.634
And so, we were just sort of
like, "Oh, we haven't recorded

00:07:26.634 --> 00:07:28.934
the drums for this one yet,
see if you wanna --"

00:07:28.934 --> 00:07:30.968
And he just played the drums
on the song.

00:07:30.968 --> 00:07:32.300
-Is that right?
-Yeah.

00:07:32.300 --> 00:07:34.701
Sometimes it's --
it just happens like that.

00:07:34.701 --> 00:07:37.467
-But did you know him before
the film "All Too Well"?

00:07:37.467 --> 00:07:38.767
-So, no, I didn't.

00:07:38.767 --> 00:07:42.467
I reached out to him to be
in the short film

00:07:42.467 --> 00:07:43.834
for "All Too Well"

00:07:43.834 --> 00:07:46.567
because I had always
really loved his work.

00:07:46.567 --> 00:07:49.133
I also felt like he seemed like
a very quick-witted person.

00:07:49.133 --> 00:07:51.567
And I knew that --
-He's funny.

00:07:51.567 --> 00:07:54.067
-I wanted the character
to be charming.

00:07:54.067 --> 00:07:58.501
I wanted -- I wanted that sort
of charming, quick, cleverness

00:07:58.501 --> 00:08:02.100
and the charisma to be
why we love the character

00:08:02.100 --> 00:08:05.033
because there's a lot
of complexity to that character

00:08:05.033 --> 00:08:06.634
that makes us
not love him as much.

00:08:06.634 --> 00:08:08.534
So I wanted Dylan
to bring something...

00:08:08.534 --> 00:08:09.868
-His layers. His -- Yeah.

00:08:09.868 --> 00:08:12.834
-...to the character
to make us, you know, love him.

00:08:12.834 --> 00:08:14.901
And I do think that was achieved
in the short film.

00:08:14.901 --> 00:08:19.267
Meanwhile, I mean, Dylan and I
become really close friends.

00:08:19.267 --> 00:08:21.434
Jack becomes really
close friends with Dylan.

00:08:21.434 --> 00:08:23.300
We all start hanging out
all the time.

00:08:23.300 --> 00:08:26.200
Like, it's the same with
Sadie Sink, who was in it.

00:08:26.200 --> 00:08:28.367
[ Cheers and applause ]
I just --

00:08:28.367 --> 00:08:30.300
I've been very lucky
to work with

00:08:30.300 --> 00:08:32.601
really nice,
really talented people.

00:08:32.601 --> 00:08:35.067
And that's not something
I ever take for granted.

00:08:35.067 --> 00:08:37.467
And a lot of the time,
we end up really good friends.

00:08:37.467 --> 00:08:39.667
-But is it because --
I mean, you've --

00:08:39.667 --> 00:08:42.133
you've done the albums,
you've done the rerecordings.

00:08:42.133 --> 00:08:45.467
But also, you haven't toured in
like four years.

00:08:45.467 --> 00:08:49.234
[ Cheers and applause ]
-I know.

00:08:49.234 --> 00:08:51.868
-We want you to.
-I think I should do it.

00:08:51.868 --> 00:08:58.701
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:08:58.701 --> 00:09:06.234
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:09:06.234 --> 00:09:08.367
-Are we talking sooner
than later?

00:09:10.367 --> 00:09:12.701
-You know, I should do it.

00:09:12.701 --> 00:09:15.767
-Yeah.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:09:15.767 --> 00:09:18.734
When it's time,
you'll let us know.

00:09:18.734 --> 00:09:21.334
-When it's time, we'll do it.

00:09:21.334 --> 00:09:23.801
[ Laughter ]
I miss it.

00:09:23.801 --> 00:09:24.901
I really miss it.

00:09:24.901 --> 00:09:28.167
I miss, you know,
when you write songs

00:09:28.167 --> 00:09:29.734
and you're proud
of the songs,

00:09:29.734 --> 00:09:32.601
and you have the fans reacting,

00:09:32.601 --> 00:09:35.901
the most potent way
that you can see them react

00:09:35.901 --> 00:09:38.467
is when you're looking
into their faces.

00:09:38.467 --> 00:09:39.901
Do you know what I mean?
I miss that a lot.

00:09:39.901 --> 00:09:41.801
I really miss that connection.

00:09:41.801 --> 00:09:43.033
-They're looking at you
right now.

00:09:43.033 --> 00:09:46.400
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:09:46.400 --> 00:09:48.167
Yeah.

00:09:48.167 --> 00:09:50.334
-So, it's like --

00:09:50.334 --> 00:09:53.934
I'm thinking something like,
it's like this but with singing.

00:09:53.934 --> 00:09:56.367
-Yeah, okay, good.

00:09:56.367 --> 00:10:00.367
By the way, I saw you received
a doctorate from NYU,

00:10:00.367 --> 00:10:02.167
and you had
a very great speech.

00:10:02.167 --> 00:10:04.868
[ Cheers and applause ]
You're a doctor.

00:10:04.868 --> 00:10:06.400
Dr. Taylor Swift.

00:10:06.400 --> 00:10:08.133
Sorry, I should have announced
you earlier as doctor.

00:10:08.133 --> 00:10:09.868
-No, it's fine.
It's fine, it's fine.

00:10:09.868 --> 00:10:10.901
I respond to many things.
-I'm sorry.

00:10:10.901 --> 00:10:12.534
I'm just getting used to it,
yeah.

00:10:12.534 --> 00:10:14.267
And you said --
You made a funny joke.

00:10:14.267 --> 00:10:15.701
You're like, "I'm a doctor,

00:10:15.701 --> 00:10:17.934
but I'm not the doctor you need
in case of an emergency.

00:10:17.934 --> 00:10:19.334
Do not call me.

00:10:19.334 --> 00:10:23.834
But you call me if you need
me to name breeds of cats."

00:10:23.834 --> 00:10:25.801
-Yeah.

00:10:25.801 --> 00:10:27.567
-"I could be a doctor at that."

00:10:27.567 --> 00:10:29.200
And you said,
"I could name like,"

00:10:29.200 --> 00:10:31.634
I don't know, 50 breeds of cats
or something like that.

00:10:31.634 --> 00:10:34.167
-Yeah, that was me committing
an act

00:10:34.167 --> 00:10:36.901
of extreme hyperbole
with the 50 --

00:10:36.901 --> 00:10:40.400
